  <h2>Licensing and forkability</h2>

  <p>
    The incident renewed a question about what the published source permits.
    On 2 August 2026,
    <a href="/record/sources/notgrubles-license-fork-question/">notgrubles asked</a>
    whether Coinkite's licence would legally prevent forking the firmware to
    provide longer-term support if the company went out of business.
    <Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="notgrubles' question about whether the firmware licence would prevent a long-term-support fork if the company folded"
      source="notgrubles, 2 Aug 2026, preserved as an X capture"
      href="/record/sources/notgrubles-license-fork-question/"
      compact
    />
    The
    question sits against an attributed history:
    <a href="/record/sources/gegelsmr4-licensing-history/">a community account posted the same day</a>
    links the firmware's Trezor-derived origins and a later relicensing under
    the Commons Clause to the outside-review question.
    <Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="The community account linking the firmware's Trezor-derived origins and Commons Clause relicensing to the outside-review question"
      source="gegelsmr4, 2 Aug 2026, preserved as an X capture"
      href="/record/sources/gegelsmr4-licensing-history/"
      compact
    />
    Neither post is a legal
    analysis, and this archive has not itself construed the licence terms; the
    question is recorded as open.
  </p>

  <h2>Follow-up items as checked on 6 August 2026</h2>

  <ul class="node-list">
    <li><strong><a href="https://x.com/nvk/status/2083216713693151552" rel="noopener nofollow">Coinkite promised a technical postmortem.</a></strong> The captured statement does not give a publication date or detailed scope.</li>
    <li><strong>No captured source announces an independent audit of the fixed firmware.</strong> This is an archive limitation, not proof that no private review exists.</li>
    <li><strong>No incident-specific CVE record was identified.</strong> NVD keyword searches for COLDCARD and Coinkite returned only the earlier <a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2019-14356" rel="noopener nofollow">CVE-2019-14356</a> OLED side-channel record. A differently worded or unpublished request could be missed.</li>
    <li><strong>The two reproduction scripts described by Gregory Sanders (instagibbs) remain unavailable here.</strong> A dated search of his public GitHub repositories, code results and gist index found no matching publication, so the archive still cannot inspect the test conditions or method.
      <Claim
        basis="unverified"
        scope="The absence of a located formal Coinkite review, incident-specific CVE, audit announcement and public instagibbs reproduction scripts"
        source="Bounded searches of the official Coinkite blog, current firmware repository, NVD keyword results and public instagibbs GitHub surfaces, checked 6 Aug 2026"
        compact
      />
    </li>
    <li><strong>A fixed synthetic Mk3 regression vector was published in this archive on 1 August.</strong> It exercises one stated device-state path and cross-checks BIP39 and BIP32 reference vectors. It is not a real-device population measurement or attack benchmark.</li>
  </ul>
